   "title": "Note: About",
   "color": "#222",
   "bgcolor": "#000",
   "properties": {
    "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
   },
   "widgets_values": [
    "## Gaussian splat I/O — transform, export, preview\n\nImage → TripoSplat → splat, then a small transform step and three ways to consume the splat.\n\n**Pipeline**\n1. `Remove Background (BiRefNet)` → `TripoSplat Preprocess Image` (1024 px).\n2. `TripoSplat Conditioning` + `KSampler` (20 steps, cfg 3.0, dpmpp_2m / simple) → `VAE Decode TripoSplat`\n   (262,144 gaussians).\n3. **`Transform Splat`**: rotate 205° on Y (turn the generated shell to face the camera) and scale 1.15 on all axes (a non-destructive check that the\n   splat is a real 3D asset, not a baked image).\n4. `Splat To File 3D` (PLY) packs the splat for the file-based nodes: **`Save Gaussian Splat`** writes the\n   native splat + viewport, **`Save GLB`** writes the packed file, **`Preview Gaussian Splat`** gives the\n   interactive viewport (the two viewers take a *file*, not the raw `SPLAT` type).\n\n**Render**: `Render Splat` draws the transformed splat to a still image so the transform is visible without\nopening a 3D viewer.\n\n**Why transform first**: downstream engines expect a specific up-axis and unit scale. Rotating/scaling here\nproves the export keeps its own coordinate frame; set `rotate_y = 0`, `scale = 1` to export as generated."
   ]
  },
